	<h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-light leading-tight tracking-tight md:text-4xl">
		Most of Your Software Process Is Cosplay
	</h1>

	<blockquote class="mt-6">
		<p class="text-sm font-medium">TL;DR</p>
		<p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			A project came in that was, honestly, 5 screens of CRUD and 1 screen reading a materialized view. The actual coding was a few days of work. Getting to
			the coding took tickets, story points, sprint ceremony, a design review, multiple environments, and a pipeline, before anyone touched an editor. That gap
			is the thing I want to talk about. Process should scale with how hard a problem actually is. Most of the time, it scales with something else: how
			anxious the organization is, and how many careers are built on owning the process instead of the output.
		</p>
	</blockquote>

	<section class="mt-10">
		<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed">
			Here's the project. Five screens: create, list, edit, view, delete. One more screen that reads from a materialized view someone already built. No
			exotic business logic, no distributed system, no third integration that changes the shape of the other two. A competent engineer, left alone, writes
			this in a few days.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			It didn't ship in a few days. It went through a backlog refinement session to turn "build 5 screens" into six tickets with story points attached. It
			went through a sprint planning meeting to decide which of those tickets fit in the sprint. It needed a design review before anyone was allowed to
			start, and a separate environment provisioned before anyone could deploy to it. A pipeline had to exist before the first commit could go anywhere.
			Standups happened daily to report progress on work that, if you just wrote it, would already be done.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			None of that made the software better. The 5 screens that came out the other end look exactly like the 5 screens a good engineer would have typed
			directly. What changed is how long it took, and how many people had to be in a room to watch it happen.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			That's the part I want to name directly: a lot of that room is cosplay. Standing in a circle holding a story-point estimate for a screen you could
			have built in the time it took to estimate it is not engineering. It's dressing up as an engineering org, for an audience that isn't even watching.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Where the ceremony actually comes from</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Agile started as a reaction to something real: the old waterfall model, where a spec got written a year before any code shipped, and by the time it
			shipped the market had moved. The original point was small, direct, and almost embarrassingly simple. Talk to the customer. Ship something working
			early. Adjust. That's it. That's the whole insight.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			What we got instead is an industry of certifications, story point estimation debates, retro facilitators, and Jira workflows with more states than
			the actual code has functions. None of that is the insight. It's a costume built around the insight, and at some point the costume became the job.
			You can tell, because teams keep doing the ceremony on projects small enough that the ceremony costs more than the work.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Cloud went the same direction. "We need infrastructure" turned into a stack of managed services, IaC modules, and multi-environment pipelines for
			apps that would run fine on a single box for years. And now the same pattern is repeating with AI. "Use the model to help you code" is turning into
			its own vocabulary of agents, harnesses, and orchestration layers, some of which earns its keep, plenty of which is people building a title around a
			tool instead of using the tool.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">sqlite and linux never had an RFC first</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			The software most engineers still admire decades later wasn't built by inventing a methodology first. Linus Torvalds wrote a kernel because he
			wanted a Unix-like system on his own hardware and the existing options didn't fit. D. Richard Hipp wrote SQLite because a client needed a database
			that didn't require running a server process. Neither of them opened with a framework for how software should be developed. They opened with a
			problem, and they solved it directly, in code, in public, and let the design get argued about afterward.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			The methodology talk came later, from people writing books and giving conference talks about how great software gets made. That's fine as
			retrospective analysis. It becomes a problem when teams start hiring the vocabulary before they've earned the right to have any, applying a
			framework built for coordinating hundreds of engineers to a task one engineer could finish before lunch.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">The same costume is showing up in the AI era</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Take the 5-CRUD-screens project again, but hand it to a team in 2026. Increasingly, the instinct isn't to open Claude Code and ask it to build the 5
			screens. It's to first stand up a multi-agent orchestration framework, a prompt-chaining pipeline, an evaluation harness, and a planning layer that
			decides which sub-agent handles which screen. By the time all of that exists, the actual task, 5 screens and a read from a materialized view, is
			still sitting there, unbuilt, now underneath another layer of scaffolding.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			For a task this size, you don't need an agent framework. You need to employ Claude directly, describe the 5 screens and the materialized view, let
			it write the code, and review the output the way you'd review a junior engineer's PR. The orchestration layer earns its place when you're running
			hundreds of agents against a real production system with real failure modes to coordinate. It's dead weight bolted onto a task one model, used
			directly, finishes in an afternoon.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			This is the same pattern as agile and cloud, just wearing a newer costume. The vocabulary changed, agents instead of sprints, harnesses instead of
			pipelines, but the tell is identical: process and tooling accumulating around a task faster than the task itself grows in difficulty. The fix is
			also identical. Ask what the scaffolding is actually for, on this specific task, before you build it.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">So is software engineering a made-up job?</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			No, and I don't actually believe that. Writing correct, maintainable code that solves a real problem is a real skill, and it's the part of the job
			that's always been real. The question worth taking seriously is narrower: does the process layer on top of that skill scale with the actual
			difficulty of the problem, or with something else?
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			My honest read, after enough projects like the 5-screen one: a meaningful chunk of it scales with organizational anxiety and career incentives.
			Anxious orgs add process because a checklist feels safer than trusting one engineer to just handle it, even when the task doesn't warrant the risk
			the checklist is defending against. And once a role exists whose output is "the process was followed" rather than "the thing shipped," that role has
			every incentive to keep the process alive, on projects that need it and on projects that don't.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Where ceremony actually earns its place</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			This isn't an argument for zero process anywhere. A migration touching a production database with no rollback path deserves a review before anyone
			runs it. A feature that a dozen teams depend on deserves a design doc, because the cost of getting the interface wrong is paid by everyone
			downstream. A regulated industry has real audit requirements that aren't optional. In each of those cases, the process exists because the blast
			radius of a mistake is genuinely large, and skipping the process would be the reckless choice, not the fast one.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			The 5-CRUD-screens project had none of that. One engineer, one small feature, no other team depending on the interface, a mistake fixable in the
			next commit. Blast radius near zero. The ceremony applied to it wasn't calibrated to the risk. It was applied because it's the default, and nobody
			stopped to ask whether this specific task needed it.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">The rule I actually use</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Match the process to the blast radius, not to the org chart. Before adding a ticket, a meeting, an environment, or a review step, ask what specific
			failure it prevents, and whether that failure is even possible on this task. If a 5-screen CRUD app fails, someone fixes it in an afternoon. If a
			production migration fails, customers lose data. Those two situations do not deserve the same number of steps between "we need this" and "it's
			live."
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			This is most of what I bring into a fractional CTO engagement: cutting the process a founder inherited from a much bigger company down to the size
			their actual risk warrants, so the engineers I work with spend their time writing the 5 screens instead of reporting on writing the 5 screens.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">The takeaway</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			If the task is genuinely 5 CRUD screens and 1 screen reading a materialized view, the honest move is to open an editor and write the 5 screens. Not
			cosplay a bigger job around it to make a few days of work feel like a quarter of work. The vocabulary changes every decade, waterfall, agile, cloud, and
			now AI and agent harnesses, but the underlying question stays the same: is this process here because the problem needs it, or because it makes the
			problem look bigger than it is?
		</p>
	</section>

